      overview: "Places Murabaha within the family of 'fiduciary sales' (Buyoo' al Amanat) that require honest cost disclosure, and surveys classical jurists' definitions.",
      simpleExplanation: "There's a family of sales where the seller must honestly state the original cost: Tawliyah (resell at exactly the same price, no profit), Wadhi'ah/Mohatah (resell at a discount below cost), and Murabaha (resell at cost plus a disclosed, agreed profit). This is different from Musawamah, the ordinary sale where price is just bargained with no cost disclosure at all.",
      academicExplanation: "Sales that become effective with express mention of the original cost are termed Buyoo' al Amanat (fiduciary sales) in Fiqh literature, comprising Tawliyah (resale at stated original price, no profit/loss), Wadhi'ah/Mohatah (resale at a discount from original cost), and Murabaha (sale with a fixed profit margin over cost) - all can be spot or deferred. Musawamah, by contrast, is an ordinary sale with price mutually agreed without any reference to the seller's cost. Murabaha derives from 'Ribh' (gain/profit/addition) and was practiced in pre-Islamic times; Imam Malik records it in Al-Muwatta. Al-Marghinani (Hanafi) defines it as 'the sale of anything for the price at which it was purchased by the seller and an addition of a fixed sum by way of profit.' Ibn Qudama (Hanbali) defines it as 'the sale at capital cost plus a known profit; the knowledge of capital cost is a precondition in it,' illustrated by the seller stating 'my capital involved in this deal is so much... and I sell it to you for this cost plus a profit of X.' Imam Malik held that Murabaha involves no credit - it is completed by simultaneous exchange of goods and price - and the Maliki school as a whole considers it Naqis (defective/disfavored) due to its demanding conditions, though not prohibited. Imam Shafi'e (in Kitabul Umm) expanded the concept to include credit transactions. Because the buyer must know the original price, expenses and profit, Murabaha is fundamentally a 'contract of trustworthiness.'",
      examExplanation: "Fiduciary sales (Buyoo' al Amanat) = Tawliyah (same price, no profit), Wadhi'ah/Mohatah (discount from cost), Murabaha (cost + disclosed profit) - all require honest cost disclosure, unlike Musawamah (bargained price, no disclosure). Classical definitions: Al-Marghinani (Hanafi) - price paid + fixed profit addition; Ibn Qudama (Hanbali) - capital cost + known profit, cost knowledge is precondition. Imam Malik: no credit involved, simultaneous exchange; Malikis consider it Naqis (defective) though not prohibited. Imam Shafi'e extended it to credit transactions.",
