      id: "ch9-t5",
      sectionNumber: "9.6",
      sectionTitle: "Specific Conditions of Murabaha",
      title: "Specific Conditions of Murabaha: Goods, Cost Disclosure and Price Certainty",
      overview: "Details the additional conditions specific to Murabaha beyond the general sale conditions: eligible goods, what expenses can be added to cost, mandatory disclosure, and the buyer's remedies if misled.",
      simpleExplanation: "On top of the general sale rules, Murabaha has extra requirements: the goods must be real (though not necessarily physical - rights can qualify) but cannot be currency, gold/silver, or debt instruments. The seller must truthfully state the original cost and can add certain genuine expenses (like transport or processing) but not fake ones, and must disclose if the original purchase was itself on credit. The profit margin, once agreed, can't be changed later (except for a supplier rebate). If the seller lies about the cost, different schools give the buyer different remedies - from an unconditional right to cancel, to requiring the seller just to refund the difference.",
      academicExplanation: "Specific conditions: (1) goods must be real but not necessarily tangible (rights/royalties qualify); (2) currency/monetary units (subject to Bai' al Sarf) cannot be sold via Murabaha since they require simultaneous exchange; (3) debt instruments/credit documents cannot be the subject of Murabaha (debt cannot be sold except via Hawalah rules, and profit on debt would be Riba); (4) the seller must truthfully state the original price and additional expenses, saying 'this article has cost me so much' (not 'I purchased this at such a rate', which could misrepresent), with schools differing on what expenses can be added to cost - Hanafis allow expenses that modify the object (tailoring, dyeing) plus incidental ones (transport, storage, commission); Malikis divide expenses into three tiers (directly cost-affecting = addable; post-profit-calculation services not self-provided = addable; services the seller could have but didn't provide = not addable); Shafi'es align with the Maliki final category; Hanbalis pragmatically allow all expenses by mutual consent if disclosed; (5) the seller must disclose all commodity aspects, defects, benefits, and critically whether the original purchase was itself on credit (since credit prices are typically higher) - a deliberately inflated original price violates Murabaha, and any rebate later received on the goods must be passed to the buyer; (6) the profit margin is mutually agreed and, once fixed, cannot be increased (except for supplier rebates, which reduce, not increase, the price); (7) the price basis cannot be Majhul (unspecified/uncertain) - it must be expressed in identical, specified units; (8) if the seller misstates the original cost, remedies vary by school: per Imam Malik, buyer may rescind unless seller refunds the difference (making the sale binding upon refund); Hanafis give an unqualified rescission option; Hanbalis treat the sale as binding once the difference is refunded; Shafi'es split between the two views; (9) the purchaser has an inherent right of Khiyar (option) even without explicit stipulation if defrauded by false statements about the article, price, expenses, or if the seller concealed that the original purchase was itself on deferred payment - though if the buyer has already used or the goods have been destroyed, Abu Hanifa and Muhammad deny any price deduction (Abu Yusuf and Ibn-abi-Laila allow deduction even then).",
      examExplanation: "Murabaha-specific conditions: goods must be real (tangibles or intangible rights, but NOT currency/gold-silver/debt instruments); seller must truthfully disclose cost + genuine expenses (schools differ on what expenses count - Hanafi broadest, Maliki/Shafi'e narrower, Hanbali most permissive with disclosure); must disclose if original purchase was itself on credit; profit margin fixed once agreed (only reducible via rebate, never increasable); price must not be Majhul (unspecified). If seller misstates cost: Maliki - buyer can rescind unless refunded; Hanafi - unqualified rescission right; Hanbali - binding once refunded; Shafi'e - split view. Buyer's Khiyar applies if defrauded, but is generally lost once goods are consumed/destroyed (per Abu Hanifa/Muhammad; Abu Yusuf/Ibn-abi-Laila disagree).",
