      sectionNumber: "9.9.1-9.9.3",
      sectionTitle: "Avoiding Buy-back / Khiyar in Murabaha / Time of Executing Murabaha",
      title: "Murabaha Issues I: Buy-back (Bai' al 'Inah), Option to Rescind, and Timing of Execution",
      overview: "Covers three practical compliance issues: avoiding Bai' al 'Inah (buy-back), whether the customer has a right to rescind for defects, and when exactly the bank may legally execute the sale.",
      simpleExplanation: "Banks must be very careful the customer isn't already the owner of the goods being 'financed' - if so, the deal is really just Bai' al 'Inah (buy-back), a disguised interest loan, which mainstream scholars reject even though some Malaysian regulators accept it. On defects: most banks don't build in a formal right to cancel (Khiyar) but instead make the customer responsible for inspecting goods themselves, while shortfalls in quantity/spec remain the seller's problem. On timing: the sale can only legally happen after the bank actually owns and possesses the goods - selling before that point undermines the whole basis of Murabaha.",
      academicExplanation: "Bai' al 'Inah ('buy-back') is a double sale where borrower and lender sell and resell an object between them - once for cash, once for a higher credit price - net economic effect of an interest loan; it is a device to circumvent the Riba prohibition and is therefore prohibited by mainstream Shari'ah experts (though Malaysian banking authorities historically accepted it, a minority position). Banks conducting MPO must verify the requested goods are not already owned by the client. On Khiyar (option to rescind): most scholars do not consider an explicit Khiyar clause necessary in modern Murabaha; banks commonly stipulate that defects become the buyer's liability if he personally examines the goods or they were adequately described to eliminate ignorance, while any shortfall in quantity/specification remains the seller's liability (proportionally reducing the price, with a rescission right). Juristically, Khiyar al 'Aib (option of defect) and Khiyar al Wasf (option of quality) remain available to the client if goods prove defective or non-conforming before Murabaha execution, and can be returned to the supplier for replacement via the same or a new Murabaha. If option is fully available to the customer, the bank bears greater risk requiring more intensive market surveys, often impractical for banks in their current state - hence practice tends toward appointing the customer as purchasing/inspection agent, with the client responsible for verifying quality themselves, backed by a performance guarantee if he nominated the supplier. On timing: Murabaha may only be legally executed after the bank has actually taken ownership and possession and become responsible for loss/defects; some edicts reportedly permit sale before the bank takes possession, which the author flags as a credibility risk for Islamic banking. Agency contracts (client buying on the bank's behalf) must remain totally separate and independent from the Murabaha sale contract itself - a distinct offer and acceptance must follow after the client, as agent, has taken possession.",
      examExplanation: "Bai' al 'Inah = prohibited double-sale buy-back (disguised interest loan); mainstream scholars reject it (Malaysia is an exception in practice). Banks must verify goods aren't pre-owned by the client. Khiyar: not usually a stipulated formal clause in modern bank Murabaha - defects become buyer's liability if he inspected/was informed, but quantity/spec shortfalls remain seller's liability (price reduction + rescission right); Khiyar al 'Aib and Khiyar al Wasf remain available if goods are defective before execution. Timing: Murabaha execution must strictly follow the bank's actual possession/ownership; agency (client buying on bank's behalf) and the Murabaha sale must be kept as separate, sequential contracts with a fresh offer/acceptance after possession.",
      keyPoints: [
        "Bai' al 'Inah ('buy-back') is a prohibited double-sale device (cash sale then higher-price credit resale between the same two parties) that produces the economic effect of an interest loan.",
        "Mainstream Shari'ah scholars from the Middle East and elsewhere reject Bai' al 'Inah; Malaysian banking authorities have historically been an exception in accepting it.",
        "Modern bank Murabaha contracts typically do not stipulate a formal Khiyar clause; instead, defect liability shifts to the buyer if he inspected the goods himself or was adequately informed, while quantity/specification shortfalls remain the seller's liability.",
        "Khiyar al 'Aib (defect option) and Khiyar al Wasf (quality option) remain juristically available to the client for defective/non-conforming goods discovered before Murabaha execution.",
        "Murabaha must be executed only after the bank has actually taken ownership and possession of the goods, becoming responsible for loss/defects - agency (purchase on the bank's behalf) and the Murabaha sale itself must remain separate, sequential contracts with distinct offer and acceptance."
      ],
      definitions: [
        { term: "Bai' al 'Inah", definition: "A double sale by which a borrower and lender sell and resell an object between themselves - once for cash and again for a higher price on credit - with the net economic result of an interest-bearing loan; a legal device to circumvent the prohibition of Riba, considered impermissible by mainstream Shari'ah experts." }
      ],
