      title: "Murabaha Issues II: Default Penalties, Early-Payment Rebates, and the Prohibition on Rollover",
      overview: "Explains how Islamic banks handle late payment (charity-directed penalties, not interest), why early-payment rebates are mostly disallowed as bank policy, and why 're-pricing' receivables (rollover) is explicit Riba.",
      simpleExplanation: "If a customer pays late, the bank can charge a penalty as a deterrent, but that money has to go to charity, not into the bank's own profit - because once the sale price is fixed, the bank has no further claim to extra money. Some scholars have gone further and allowed banks to keep late fees calculated as if the money had earned a return during the delay - but the book flags this as dangerously close to conventional interest. If a customer pays early, most scholars don't allow the bank to give a discount, though AAOIFI permits a rebate if it wasn't promised in the contract, at the bank's discretion. And 'rollover' - adding a new profit margin onto an unpaid Murabaha instead of just rescheduling - is explicit Riba, and flatly not allowed.",
      academicExplanation: "On defaults: unlike conventional banks (which reschedule debt at a higher rate), Islamic banks stipulate an acceleration clause (all remaining instalments become due upon unjustified delay) as a disciplinary device. Contemporary Shari'ah scholars have reached consensus that banks may impose late fees on delinquent clients (genuinely unable clients being exempted), but such penalty proceeds must go to charity - only a court or independent body can allocate any part as solatium (compensation) to the bank, per the Shariat Appellate Bench of Pakistan's Supreme Court (which allows legislatures to empower courts to award solatium from penalties, with the remainder for charitable/public-interest use). A more expansive Fatwah (Al Baraka Sudan) authorizes banks to treat late fees as their own income, calculated on the profit rate actually realized by the bank during the delay period (as if the delayed money had itself earned that return) - but the author cautions this is uncomfortably close to conventional opportunity-cost-based interest and threatens the credibility of the distinction between Islamic and Western trade financing. On early payment: the majority of contemporary scholars (OIC Fiqh Academy, Pakistan's Shariat Appellate Bench, Middle Eastern Shari'ah committees) do not permit a rebate for early payment in bank Murabaha, viewing it as functionally similar to conventional interest-based instalment discounting. AAOIFI's Murabaha Standard, however, allows a rebate if not already stipulated in the contract - purely at the bank's discretion, decided case-by-case with the Shari'ah advisor, never as routine practice. On rollover: 'rollover' means booking a fresh Murabaha against the unpaid receivable of a prior Murabaha, adding further mark-up to the defaulted amount - this is explicit Riba, since the bank has no right to re-price a receivable once ownership of the sold goods has already transferred to the client (the bank's claim is fixed at the original agreed price). Rescheduling (extending the payment period without increasing the amount) is allowed; re-pricing (rollover) is not. Any late-payment charge collected per the client's own undertaking goes to charity, not bank income; a genuinely fresh Murabaha (new goods) remains possible.",
      examExplanation: "Default: acceleration clause allowed (delay triggers full amount due); late fees allowed by consensus but must go to CHARITY, not bank profit (court/independent body may award partial solatium to bank); the Al Baraka Sudan Fatwah allowing banks to keep late fees as realized-profit-rate income is flagged as risky/interest-like by the author. Early payment: majority view disallows rebates (too interest-like); AAOIFI permits a discretionary, non-stipulated rebate. Rollover: booking a NEW Murabaha with added markup against an unpaid OLD Murabaha receivable = explicit Riba (bank cannot re-price a fixed receivable); rescheduling (same amount, later date) IS allowed; rollover (re-pricing) is NOT.",
      keyPoints: [
        "Islamic banks may use an acceleration clause (full balance due on unjustified delay) as a discipline mechanism, since re-pricing/interest-style penalties are not available.",
        "Late-payment penalties are permissible by contemporary scholarly consensus, but proceeds must go to charity, not bank profit - only a court/independent body can allocate any portion as solatium (compensation) to the bank.",
        "The Al Baraka Sudan Fatwah permitting banks to retain late fees calculated on their realized profit rate during the delay period is presented critically - the author warns it resembles conventional opportunity-cost-based interest.",
        "The majority of contemporary scholars disallow early-payment rebates in bank Murabaha (too similar to conventional discount-for-early-payment financing); AAOIFI allows a non-stipulated, purely discretionary rebate.",
        "'Rollover' - booking a new Murabaha with added markup against a prior unpaid Murabaha receivable - is explicit Riba, because the bank has no right to re-price a receivable once the sold goods' ownership has already transferred.",
        "Rescheduling (extending the payment date without increasing the amount owed) is permitted; rollover (re-pricing/adding markup) is not."
      ],
